emulate link(2) under a double SIGSYS, and stop the linkat emulation from moving the file

Android's zygote seccomp policy for apps traps plain link() *and*
linkat().  dpkg creates its /var/lib/dpkg/status-old backup with a plain
link(status, status-old), so under the app policy the flow is:

  link() SIGSYS -> handler rewrites the registers to the linkat layout
  and restarts -> the restarted linkat SIGSYSes too -> handler runs the
  PR_linkat emulation with the wrong registers.

At that second SIGSYS the registers CURRENT hold the *restored link()
layout* (push_specific_regs restored them from ORIGINAL_SECCOMP_REWRITE
at the syscall-exit stop of the rewritten call), so the emulation read
newdirfd from SYSARG_3 = a stack pointer, newpath from SYSARG_4 =
whatever the caller of link() happened to leave there, and translate_path
returned -EBADF.  dpkg died on "status-old: Permission denied" and
apt-get install exited 100 - the failure that ended the Ubuntu E2E run
35035691404 after apt-get update had finally passed.

The olddirfd = peek_reg(tracee, ORIGINAL, SYSARG_1) read was equally
wrong: for a direct guest linkat() the SIGSYS arrives with no prior
sysenter stop of that call, so ORIGINAL holds whatever syscall ran
before, and even for the rewritten link() ORIGINAL holds the linkat
layout saved at the restart's sysenter - which is not what a link-layout
SYSARG_1 means.

Two more defects in the same emulation, found while fixing the first:

  1. It called renameat() and, on the EXDEV/EACCES fallback path,
     unlink()ed the source - it *moved* the file instead of hardlinking
     it.  Every emulated link() destroyed its source: dpkg would have
     lost /var/lib/dpkg/status itself once the register bug was fixed.
     The kernel runs this code inside the same seccomp filter that
     trapped linkat, so the host link(2) would SIGSYS-kill proot; a byte
     copy is the only emulation that can run.  O_CREAT|O_EXCL reproduces
     link(2)'s EEXIST-on-existing-target errno.
  2. flags was read from a register that is garbage in both flows (it
     was never used, so this is cosmetic).

The fix: PR_link sets tracee->link_rewrite_pending before restarting;
PR_linkat consumes the flag to know CURRENT holds the link() layout
(SYSARG_1/2 = old/new path) versus a real linkat() layout, and both
flows then translate the paths and byte-copy the source to the target,
keeping the source and failing EEXIST when the target exists.

Validated on x86_64 Linux with a seccomp filter that TRAPs __NR_link and
__NR_linkat exactly like the Android app policy: link() creates
status-old with identical bytes and keeps the source; link() onto an
existing name fails EEXIST; a missing source fails ENOENT; a direct
linkat(AT_FDCWD,...) creates the target and keeps the source; linkat()
onto an existing name fails EEXIST; untrapped link/linkat still work
natively; the rename-SIGSYS fix from patch 0001 is unregressed.  Under
the same trap, in a jammy rootfs: dpkg --configure -a exits 0 and
"apt-get install -y hello" unpacks and configures the package with
status-old created and hello running - the exact flow that failed in
the E2E run.

Not upstreamed: no write access to oonid/pr; this patch is applied to
the pinned fork tarball by fetchLinuxSource in linux/build.gradle.kts
on top of 0001 and 0002.
--- a/src/proot/src/tracee/tracee.h
+++ b/src/proot/src/tracee/tracee.h
@@ -161,6 +161,11 @@
 	bool _regs_were_changed;
 	bool restore_original_regs;
 	bool restore_original_regs_after_seccomp_event;
+	/* True between a PR_link SIGSYS rewritten to linkat and the SIGSYS
+	 * of that rewritten linkat (Android traps both).  It tells the
+	 * PR_linkat emulation that CURRENT holds the restored link() layout,
+	 * not a linkat() layout.  */
+	bool link_rewrite_pending;
 
 	/* State for the special handling of SIGSTOP.  */
 	enum {
--- a/src/proot/src/tracee/seccomp.c
+++ b/src/proot/src/tracee/seccomp.c
@@ -205,6 +205,10 @@
